Debian 13 testing packages on Open Build Service
==============================================

Copyright 2026 Qore Technologies, s.r.o.

The testing project is home:davidnichols:qore:testing:debian on
https://build.opensuse.org/. Its Debian_13 repository builds native x86_64
(Debian amd64) and aarch64 (Debian arm64) packages. Ubuntu packages remain in
the qore-packagers/qore-testing Launchpad PPA. Neither staging qualification
nor publication of development snapshots constitutes a final Qore release.

Installing testing snapshots
----------------------------

The Debian 13 repository supports amd64 and arm64. Configure it only on Debian
13 (trixie); Ubuntu users should use the qore-packagers/qore-testing PPA.
Install the repository key with its pinned fingerprint, then add the signed
binary and source indexes:

    sudo apt-get install ca-certificates curl gnupg
    (
        set -eu
        qore_key_file=$(mktemp)
        trap 'rm -f "$qore_key_file"' EXIT
        curl -fsSL -o "$qore_key_file" \
            https://download.opensuse.org/repositories/home:/davidnichols:/qore:/testing:/debian/Debian_13/Release.key
        test "$(gpg --batch --show-keys --with-colons "$qore_key_file" \
            | awk -F: '$1 == "pub" { primary = 1 } $1 == "fpr" && primary { print $10; primary = 0 }')" \
            = 14BE051DACCE33336EEC09D5361BDFBA77CA501E
        sudo install -D -m 0644 "$qore_key_file" \
            /etc/apt/keyrings/qore-debian-testing.asc
    )
    printf '%s\n' \
        'Types: deb deb-src' \
        'URIs: https://download.opensuse.org/repositories/home:/davidnichols:/qore:/testing:/debian/Debian_13/' \
        'Suites: /' \
        'Architectures: amd64 arm64' \
        'Signed-By: /etc/apt/keyrings/qore-debian-testing.asc' \
        | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/qore-testing.sources >/dev/null
    sudo apt-get update
    sudo apt-get install qore

Install libqore-dev for the SDK. Independent integrations have separate
packages, for example qore-python-module, qore-v8-module and qore-xml-module.
JSON, YAML and the other bundled modules are included with Qore. Source
packages can be retrieved with apt-get source, for example:

    apt-get source qore

During a new publication, a download mirror or CDN can serve an older
Packages.gz alongside a newer signed Release file. If APT reports an unexpected
size or checksum, wait for synchronization and retry apt-get update. Keep
signature and checksum verification enabled. Publication is accepted only
after the ordinary repository URL passes those checks and native installation
and upgrade tests.

Source imports and publication
------------------------------

Import the exact locally qualified .dsc, Debian tarball and every orig source
component, checking their SHA-256 values against the qualification manifest.
Do not regenerate sources from a moving branch during an import. Record the
OBS source revision, qualified Git commit, source hashes and native build
results. Source archives remain available alongside the binary packages.

Keep publication disabled at both project and package level until acceptance
of the actual OBS outputs. The project defaults to builds disabled; enable
each qualified source package explicitly for the two supported architectures.
Set package hardware constraints appropriate to its measured build memory and
disk requirements. An OBS commit starts the package build independently of
Git CI; use [skip ci] on Git packaging changes not exercised by Git CI.

The project configuration uses:

    Type: dsc
    BinaryType: deb
    Repotype: debian
    PublishFlags: archsync noearlypublish nofailedpackages
    Keep: libuv1t64 node-acorn nodejs libnode115 libnode127 libllhttp9.3
    Prefer: libjpeg-dev

Keep preserves the build tools' installed runtime inputs while building their
replacements. CMake needs libuv1t64 when building libuv itself; Debian's Node
runtime needs node-acorn when building Acorn. Node itself uses the installed
Node executable to bundle its JavaScript inputs before compiling its replacement.
Retain nodejs and its shared library too: Debian 13 provides libnode115 and
the qualified Node 22 backport provides libnode127. Both must be eligible so
later rebuilds can use the already-built backport as their bootstrap runtime.
The Node 22 backport also links libllhttp9.3. Retain that library while
rebuilding llhttp and its bootstrap source package; otherwise Node cannot
start, and dh-nodejs reports installed JavaScript modules as missing.
Without these entries, OBS
removes the package's own output from the build environment and the tool
fails before compilation. Node reports "Cannot load externalized builtin"
for Acorn, which nodepath hides behind misleading missing-dependency errors.
Confirm each runtime input remains in the corresponding OBS _buildinfo.
The source recipes and upstream tests stay intact.

Debian dependency selection
---------------------------

Repository paths use Debian:13/security, update, standard, then backports.
The official trixie-backports compiler packages provide Clang 21 for PDFium;
record and qualify their exact versions separately from the stable packages.

OBS resolves duplicate package names by repository order. This differs from
APT's highest-candidate-version selection: an older security or updates copy
can hide a newer point-release package. Putting standard first instead can
hide security updates. Neither ordering alone establishes version parity.

Prefer libjpeg-dev when resolving the JPEG development dependency. Both that
Debian metapackage and libjpeg62-turbo-dev satisfy the name, which OBS otherwise
reports as an ambiguous choice for Cairo, ImageMagick and PROJ. The metapackage
depends on libjpeg62-turbo-dev, matching the qualified native APT environments.

The build-only debian-point-release-base package aggregates selected original
Debian:13/standard/_repository binaries whose point-release versions supersede
the earlier copies. Its _aggregate uses resign="false", explicit binary names
and a standard-to-Debian_13 repository mapping. It is never published. Verify
each aggregated binary hash against authenticated Debian APT metadata on both
architectures, and retain the original source-package provenance.

Keep related binaries at compatible versions when extending the aggregate.
For OpenSSH, select openssh-client, openssh-server and openssh-sftp-server
together: the servers require an exactly matching client version. Aggregating
only the client leaves SSH2's loopback test dependencies unresolvable when OBS
selects an older server from the higher-priority security repository.

Before accepting builds, compare every selected stable-distribution build
dependency from OBS _buildinfo with an authenticated, current native APT
candidate. Include aggregated binaries in that comparison. Missing or empty
inventories and unresolved build graphs are pending checks, not successes.
Refresh or remove aggregate entries when Debian security updates overtake
them; rebuild affected packages and recheck the complete graph. Do not reuse
a previous successful comparison after dependencies change.

The llhttp/Node dependency cycle uses a separate llhttp-bootstrap source-only
build. It retains the qualified original source archives and Debian control
file, omits Build-Depends-Arch only from the bootstrap scheduler .dsc, and
invokes dpkg-buildpackage -A. Compare the generated libllhttp-source files
with the full native llhttp build on both CPUs before accepting the cycle.
Bootstrap packages are build inputs, not a separate user-facing release.

Acceptance and evidence
------------------------

A successful OBS build is one qualification step. Download its exact packages
and retain the build log, _buildinfo, source revision and artifact hashes.
Check package contents, architecture, dependencies, hardening, detached debug
symbols and source/binary Lintian results. Run installed runtime/SDK tests in
fresh native Debian environments without source-tree module paths, including
package removal/reinstallation and the combined independent-module tests.
Revalidate artifacts after dependency-triggered rebuilds.

Qualify Qore and UUID first, then the remaining independent modules against
the OBS SDK. Bundled modules, including JSON and YAML, remain in the Qore
source package. Oracle's proprietary dependency hosting approval and the
optional V8 app catalogue's schema licensing remain separate publication
gates; local qualification does not remove either requirement.

Complete artifact and native installed-package acceptance before enabling APT
publication. For the initial publication, keep the project publish flag
disabled while removing publish overrides from the accepted source packages.
Keep explicit publish-disable overrides on build-only sources, including
debian-point-release-base and llhttp-bootstrap. Then enable publication once
at project level. Package-level publish-enable overrides take precedence over
a disabled project flag and can expose intermediate, incomplete indexes while
the package flags are being updated. Mirrors can retain those earlier indexes
after the final Release file appears.

Once OBS generates the repository, verify its signature,
architecture indexes, install/upgrade behavior and exact package hashes before
advertising it as ready to use. A local unsigned qualification repository does
not establish that the published signature or indexes are correct. Retain source
archives, logs, manifests and qualification reports in an independent durable
backup. Completed local build containers/images can then be removed; keep
the qualified artifacts and evidence. Never archive authentication tokens,
private signing keys or desktop keyring contents with package evidence.

Published versions and rebuilds
-------------------------------

Retain an acceptance manifest with every published binary's package name,
version, architecture and SHA-256, together with the matching source revision.
Never replace a published package/version/architecture tuple with different
bytes. A dependency-triggered rebuild can change the binary even when its
source is unchanged; increment the Debian revision, rebuild and qualify that
update before publication. Byte-identical rebuilds can reuse their existing
acceptance evidence after verifying the complete hashes.

The current .dsc recipes preserve their explicit Debian changelog versions.
OBS does not automatically increase Debian revisions without its optional
DEBTRANSFORM-RELEASE mechanism; see the Debian section of the OBS User Guide:
https://openbuildservice.org/help/manuals/obs-user-guide/cha-obs-package-formats
Use a separate unpublished build project for subsequent qualification, or
freeze builds in the publication project. Account for package-level build
enable rules as well as the project default. Do not leave unrestricted
dependency rebuilds feeding a public repository under unchanged versions.

Record the exact Architecture: all artifact selected for publication.
Documented semantic equivalence between two architecture-independent outputs
does not permit silently changing the hash of an already published package.
